**2. Consider a population of normally distributed scores with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Write a script to draw 1,000 random samples, each of size 40, from this population. Calculate and store each mean in a vector of means. Compare the mean and standard deviation of the population with the mean and standard deviation of the vector of means. What statistical characteristics of the mean do these comparisons illustrate? (Hint: You will need to initialize a vector object by assigning it *NULL* prior to using it in a loop.)**

```{r}
# Set up the population parameters.

pop_mean <- 100
pop_sd <- 15

# Parameters for sampling.

n_samples <- 1000
sample_size <- 40

# Vector to store the means.

sample_means <- NULL

# Draw 1,000 random samples from the population and store their means.

for (i in 1:n_samples) {
  
  # Draw a random sample of size 40.
  
  sample_data <- rnorm(sample_size, mean = pop_mean, sd = pop_sd)
  
  # Calculate the mean of this sample and store it.
  
  sample_means[i] <- mean(sample_data)
}

# Compare the population mean/sd with the mean/sd of the sample means.

cat("Population Mean:", pop_mean, "\n")
cat("Population SD:", pop_sd, "\n\n")

cat("Mean of sample means:", mean(sample_means), "\n")
cat("SD of sample means:", sd(sample_means), "\n")

# Theoretical SD of sample means.

cat("Theoretical SD of the sampling distribution:",
    pop_sd / sqrt(sample_size), "\n")


# These statistics show that the theoretical SD of the sampling distribution is, when rounding, the same as the SD of sample means which tells us the individual sample means cluster more tightly around the true mean  as sample size increases, which is the Central Limit Theorem. The mean of the sample means, or Mu, is very close to the population mean which tells us that the sample mean is an unbiased estimator of the population mean. 

```


